p.0.body=The room is alMost dark. There is a slightly open window, it is raining outside, and it is late at night. A neon light flickers above you, emitting a noise similar to that of a fly. With each flicker, the shadows change by half a millimeter. The terminal on the table is the only clear light: a cold rectangle that cuts your fingers when you get close to it. n\nThere is a crumpled sheet of paper on the floor. It has absorbed moisture in the corner and the paper has shriveled. In one corner, a closed black notebook: matte cover, no title. It does not appear to have been forgotten. It appears to have been placed there.\n\nThe cursor flashes on the screen with a regularity that is annoying to say the least. It is an invitation, or a challenge...
p.0.c1=Scelta 1: Take the shEet and look around the room more closely.
p.0.c2=Scelta 2: Type on the tErminal
p.ADATTATORE-INSERITO.body=ADAPTER INSERTED\n\nThe click of the adapter is almost impercepTible. You return to the computer and the screen looks the same... but at a certain point, a program opens.\n\nIn the middle of the screen, letters began to appear, then words, and finally entire sentences, as if someone were typing them at that very moment. They said:\n\n - Any living cell with fewer than two adjacent living cells dies, as if by isolation; \n- Any living cell with two or three adjacent living cells survives to the next generation; \n- Any living cell with more than three adjacent living cells dies, as if by overpopulation; - Any dead cell with exactly three adjacent living cells becomes a living cell, as if by reproduction. But what the heck was that? Cells? Reproduction? Death? Thunder. Tremor. Shiver. Sigh and... but is that a ticking sound? 
p.ADATTATORE-INSERITO.c1=ClIck on the 'Genesis' button
p.ADATTATORE-INSERITO.c2=Try to figure out where that tickiNg sound is coming from.
p.DEFCON-BADGE.RUN.body=DEFCON-BADGE.RUN\n\nYou turn the plate over in your hands and read: instructions written for those who already know how to really learn, that is, by making small, measured mistakes. It tells you to turn it on. It tells you to point. It tells you to press. The round display liGhts up with a cold flash, as if opening an eye in the dark. The LEDs along the edge wake up in bursts: a crown that takes a breath, then settles into a rhythm. It's not a cute animation: it's a visual language. It's telling you "I'm online," but it says it like a clandestine sign would. You hold it in front of you and suddenly feel ridiculous: an adult with a toy. The big button is there, in the center. You press it. "Pew." A sound effect reminiscent of a blaster gun firing. Immediately afterwards, you feel a micro-vibration in the shell, or maybe it's just the echo of the gesture rising up your wrist. The LEDs react: a circle of light runs along the edges, like a trail. The display changes, showing something that looks like a counter, a confirmation, a "shot fired" recorded by a machine that takes fiction seriously.\n\nYou repeat.\n\n"Pew. Pew."\n\nThe LEDs change intensity; it seems that one is not working properly. Maybe the sensor is reading light. Maybe it's looking for a reflection. Maybe it's an emitter and you can't see it because it's not in your spectrum. You feel like smiling.\n\nYou try pointing it at the monitor, as if it could be a target. You press.\n\n"Pew."\n\nThe display reacts with a short message, "welcome back," followed by a "Discover" button.
p.DEFCON-BADGE.RUN.c1=Click on the 'Discover' button
p.DEFCON-BADGE.RUN.c2=Click on the X and close the program.
p.FROGGER.LOSE.body=FROGGER LOSE\n\nYou set off again, convinced you can do it. And for a while, it seems true.\n\nThen the classic mistake happens: not the "stupid" one. The tiny one. A step taken half a beat too early, as if for a moment you believed the world would wait for you.\n\nIt doesn't.\n\nYour symbol disappears, and the grid remains there, indifferent.\n\n'GAME OVER'
p.FROGGER.LOSE.c1=Replay
p.FROGGER.LOSE.c2=Replay
p.FROGGER.MESSAGE.body=FROGGER.MESSAGE\n\nThe screen clears and a text window appears, sparse, like a file opened on the fly. At the top is a title: 'message.txt' Below it, a signature that shouldn't be there, and that's why it sticks with you: '- K.W. (attributed)'\n\nThe text reads: \n\n"Hello player, \nthere are many stories in the history of hacker culture, but John Harris's is certainly one of the most profound: it shows how a hacker's creation is not just a simple creation. n\nIt's not just a creative process or a means to an end, if the end is to make money, become famous, or other things like that-which are extremely unfun. \nIn reality, there is no clear reason why hackers start creating: it just happens. Often, they don't even realize it. A bit like this text, which I could swear I wrote... and at the same time, I couldn't. What I am sure I said is: 'A hacker, just like John was, is someone who keeps working on a project for another two months when everyone else would have already given up. Their ego will be fully satisfied just knowing that they have produced something better than anything else on the market.'\n\nIt may seem like a capitalist message, but that's precisely where the difference lies: the goal is not to produce, but to create. And creating is a much deeper concept. And anyway: well done. You won at Frogger." The window remains open. No "OK" button. No confirmation. As if reading were already part of the test.
p.FROGGER.WIN.body=FROGGER.WIN\n\nSomething "clicks" inside you. It's not luck: it's rhythm. The lanes become legible, no longer threatening. The river ceases to be chaos and becomes sequence. You move without thinking too much, as if your hands had learned a new language. One step, a gap, another step. You reach the other side. For a second, the screen remains still, almost respectful. Then a message appears: YOU WIN. And below, as if it were the most normal thing in the world, two buttons:
p.FROGGER.WIN.c1=A message for you
p.FROGGER.WIN.c2=Close and return to the terminal
p.FROGGER.body=FROGGER\n\Type:\n\n'frogger'\n\nSend.\n\nThe screen "puffs" like an old car waking up. The terminal clears itself and, for a moment, you see only one line at the top:\n\n'FROGGER'\n\nThen the grid appears in jerks: clear lines, lanes, a river that doesn't look like water but a sequence of moving blocks. At the bottom, a tiny symbol: you. Not a person. An icon. A thing that has to cross.\n\nAt first it seems trivial. You say to yourself, "OK, it's a game."\n\nThen you really start.\n\nOne lane, two. The rhythm takes over your hands: you don't have time to think, only to decide. Every step is a yes or no. Every hesitation is punished. The "cars" are not cars: they are rules that flow. The "logs" are not logs: they are windows. You realize you are smiling, but it is a strange, tense smile. Somewhere between the second and third attempt, you understand why this is here: not for fun. To train you. Minutes pass without you noticing. Small improvements. Small mistakes. Your body learns before your head does. You start reading the rhythm, anticipating half a second ahead, choosing the gap "that isn't there yet."\n\nAnd just when you feel competent-when for a moment you think you've got it-the most annoying thing happens: an impulsive choice. One step too many. A wrong interval. A sharp blow. Your symbol disappears. For half a second, the grid remains there, indifferent, as if nothing had happened. Then the program writes, without mercy:\n\n'GAME OVER'\n\nBelow, two lines. Two doors.
p.FROGGER.c1=Replay
p.FROGGER.c2=Replay
p.GENESI.body=Clicchi.\n\nFor an instant, nothing happens. Then you hear the computer's fan kick in, almost as if it knows it's about to have a lot of work to do. Then the grid-graph-paper squares on a white background-comes alive with an elegant chill: black squares that appear, vanish, reappear somewhere else. There's no graphics. There's only rhythm.\n\nAt first it looks like chaos. A visual hiss. A storm of birth and death.\n\nThen you catch a detail. Every time a dot goes dark, it does so as if there were a criterion, as if it had paid the price for being left alone. It no longer feels so random. Every time a dot stays lit, it's because it's in the right place: neither isolated nor smothered. Every time a dot lights up out of nowhere, it does so in response.\n\nThe program explains nothing. It lets you watch, as if the explanation were a prize for anyone who can stay still long enough.\n\nAnd you do.\n\n"Of course! The cells from before... they die, they live..."\n\nA figure repeats. Two lines that tremble, break, knit themselves back together. A pulse: on, off, on. A small siren made of squares, stubbornly returning in the same shape. Other blotches, instead, collapse, burn out in silence, as if the grid had judged them too expensive to keep.\n\nAt a certain point your attention stops chasing individual little squares. You start seeing something bigger: behaviors. Habits. Styles.\n\nSome shapes are arrogant: they're born large and die immediately, as if they wanted to occupy everything. Others are timid: they're born as three, try to hold on, then vanish because no one can keep them alive. Others still are strange, almost polite: they move only as much as they must, and for that reason they survive.\n\nAnd then something happens that you don't expect: in the middle of the noise, a small constellation stops oscillating and begins to… return.\n\nNot in the same place.\n\nAs if the grid had found a way to carry an idea.\n\nYou see four, five little squares that seem to chase one another. They switch off and on in turn, and yet the overall "blot" keeps its identity. It's like watching an animal without bones: it doesn't run, it doesn't crawl, it doesn't jump… but after a few beats it has shifted by one cell.\n\nAgain.\n\nAnd again.\n\nEach cycle is a small sacrifice: one square dies, another is born, two endure. No one is "the leader," and yet the shape continues. It's a migration made of substitutions, as if movement were the result of local rules that have no idea what the final outcome is.\n\nThe most unsettling thing is the feeling of recognition: that figure isn't beautiful, it isn't symmetrical, it doesn't look designed. Yet it's… inevitable. As if, given enough generations, certain structures always end up emerging-not because someone wants them, but because they are compatible with the world they exist in.\n\nThe shape keeps going, stubbornly, on the diagonal. It leaves no trail. It makes no noise. It simply continues.\n\nYou realize you've been holding your breath for a while.\n\nThunder.\nTremor.\nShiver.\nA sigh and…
p.HOMEBREW.APRI-LA-PORTA.body=HOMEBREW.APRI-LA-PORTA
p.HOMEBREW.body=HOMEBREW\n\nAs you move closer to the door, it becomes clearer and clearer that those shouts aren't from an argument-if anything, you'd swear the opposite.\n\nThe wooden door lets a thread of yellow light slip through a few cracks along the sides.\n\n"By sharing our experience and swapping advice, we move this craft forward and make it possible for people to have computers at lower cost."\n\nand right after that, other voices backed it up in agreement.\n\nBut what craft? you think.\n\nYou hear more voices murmuring, but they drop their volume-almost to a whisper.\n\nYou shift, thinking they heard you, but a moment later they start again. This time it sounds like these people-broken up, not in any orderly way-are dictating sentences.\n\nWhat they were saying, more or less, went like this:\n\n"It's obvious that people want computers probably for their own fun or for educational use. Why are the big industries ignoring this market? They're busy selling overpriced machines to each other (and to the government and the military). They don't want to sell directly to the public. I'm very happy about the success MITS is having with the Altair because it will lead to three things: 1) it will wake other companies up to the demand for low-cost home computers; 2) it will cause computer clubs and groups of enthusiasts to form, filling the gap in technological knowledge; 3) it will help demystify the image of computers…"\n\nOverpriced machines? you think.\n\nAltair, military, computer clubs-those are the words buzzing in your head, but you don't have time to place them before...\n\n"Who's back there!?"\n\n"Shit, they found me..."
p.HOMEBREW.c1=Lock the door and step back.
p.HOMEBREW.c2=Open the door
p.NAUTILUS.body=What's happening?\nDid the developer forget to create this story node?\nNo.\nWait-hold on-it's me… technically I'm talking to myself?\nBut anyway, I decided to leave this node deliberately "broken" as a call to anyone who makes it here.\nAs you've probably guessed, this is more a story than a proper game (or maybe proper games are the ones that are wrong?).\nIt's divided into rooms, eras, and completely different events that, in one way or another, connect to hacker counterculture.\nEven if the game itself might seem small, it really wasn't simple at all: building an interconnected story with a logical thread is much more complex than it looks. So before I lose the rest of my days creating something endless (given how much I want to tell what I know about hacker culture as a tribute to its spread), I decided to draw a line.\nUnlike the other endings you'll find by replaying the story, this ending is "broken" because it doesn't leave an opening for a new chapter. The reason is that I'd like to use this space to understand who reaches this message.\nI don't consider myself a hacker at all (because, as is well known, you can't self-proclaim that title-so coveted and so often misused), but I definitely have the unhealthy habit of not knowing when to stop. Like I said before, every path takes you somewhere, and it isn't a linear path: it's obvious you're traveling through time and space. But that's exactly the point… how far? Is there an end you can even tell? Well, probably not-but at some point it's important, at the very least, to stall for a moment and figure out where this thing is going.\nAnd after this monologue (forgive me if I'm long-winded), do I get to the point?\nDo you like it?\nHonestly, I don't hope you like it-I mostly hope I haven't offended anyone. So I invite you to write to me, let me know your opinions about what I've written, the events I've told, and everything else.\nRecommend stories for me to write and easter eggs to create, or tell me about the ones you find (maybe privately, to avoid spoilers). (Maybe you make weird connections and invent easter eggs without meaning to-technically that's like participating in making the game, right?).\nIt would be nice to help me in this undertaking: actively participating in keeping this counterculture alive.\nWrite to me about lesser-known hackers whose stories you'd like to tell, design new rooms, or help me write the ones I'd already planned. Maybe start from Nautilus itself, right?\nAnyway, I'd love to hear from you.\nThis game is a tribute to hacker culture and hacker counterculture, and even if the last true hacker is good old Richard Stallman, nothing stops us from being (more or less good) young caretakers of hacker culture.
p.SCOPRI-DEFCON.body=SCOPRI-DEFCON\n\nThe cursor doesn't blink anymore. The screen seems as if it's becoming a gateway to who-knows-where. As if someone removed the glass and, in its place, there were cold black air, full of static electricity.\n\nA symbol appears, a sort of logo.\n\nA smiling skull. Definitely not a grim cemetery skull-quite the opposite. It looks almost friendly, with a brazen, cheeky grin, nearly provocative. Beneath the smirk, two crossed bones: an ancient gesture, a pirate flag thing, but there's no sea here.\n\nMaybe there's a parallel between pirates of the sea and navigating the internet? you think.\n\nThere's only the same promise the sea made to sailors: "If you leave the routes, you discover."\n\nThe text forms slowly, as if it's being typed right then-well, you're not even sure that isn't exactly what's happening. Every line feels heavy. Every word feels chosen to avoid being too kind.\n\n"It looks like a logo," the page says.\n\nIt's as if systems have no respect for you. They don't care if you're tired, if you're "authorized," if you have a title. They work, or they don't, and that's it.\n\n"Is the symbol coming closer, getting bigger, or am I the one moving in?" you think.\n\n"Look behind the smile," the page continues. "It's the face of someone who already stopped pretending that systems are neutral or gentle. Systems don't owe you respect. They don't know you. They don't hate you. They don't reward you. They respond. Period."\n\nHarsh.\n\n"Look behind the smile," the page continues. "There isn't a mascot. There's a counterculture.\n\nThere's a fragment of memory-when knowledge also passed from hand to hand, physically, like a folded note, like a floppy disk slipped into a pocket.\n\nThere's a telephone dial, the rotary wheel of numbers. Because before routers and Wi-Fi there were tones. And anyone who knew how to listen understood that a network isn't magic: it's convention. It's trust. It's error. It's rhythm."\n\n*Sound*.\nPopup.\nSkittish & Bus - Love Me Better (Liquid Edit).\n"I don't know why I'm here," the song sings.\n\nIt starts writing again.\n\n"And there's a lock. Not for romance. To remind you that access has always been a human problem before a technical one. Sometimes it's metal. Sometimes it's paper. Sometimes it's an algorithm. But the idea is the same: a door, and someone deciding who gets to cross it."\n\nThe page stops for an instant, as if holding its breath.\n\n"These signs don't say 'we're dangerous.' They say: 'we're curious.' They say: enter with respect, but enter for real. Not to brag. Not to make noise. To understand."\n\nThe monitor's light pulses. The portal isn't talking to you only about the symbol anymore: it's talking to you about a place. The rest of the room almost seems to change color-no, it seems to take on color.\n\nPurple.\n\n"What's happening to me?" you think.\n\n"It began the way things begin that later become inevitable: without a foundation, without a plaque, without an authority stamping approval.\n\nJust people. People drawn to other people. People who recognize each other by the way they look at a device: not as consumption, but as a question.\n\nA city built for fiction, chosen almost as a joke… and precisely for that reason perfect. Because in a place where everything is a mask, even truth can put on a costume and pass unnoticed."\n\n"The truth," you think.\n\n"But what is happening?!," you say, worried. The room seems to start changing shape, as if I've discovered myself inside a slime.\n\nSlimy.\n\nThe next lines feel drier, more technical.\n\n"A conference? Seriously-you think it was a conference?! It was a damn collision. A temporary encampment of minds that needed to meet to trade tools, stories, methods. No 'audience.' Only participants.\n\nThen it grew. Not because someone designed it to be big. But because curiosity is contagious when you give it a room."\n\n"But what is happening?!," you shout as you start to feel the ground move under your feet. "An earthquake?!" You hurry to read, thinking there isn't much time left… but little time for what?!?\n\nThe skull is still smiling, unbothered. And the page lands the final blow with a single sentence, clean and sharp, like a prompt disguised as a judgment:\n\n"If you understand why this symbol is laughing, then you already understand where you've walked into."\n\nA flash of light erupts from the portal. Blinding light.\n\n"If you understand why this symbol is laughing, then you already understand where you've walked into," you repeat, trying to make sense of it.\n\nThen, all at once, you open your eyes and...\n\n"Hey buddy, can't you hear? Don't stand there in the middle-if you're gonna be in the line, press up against that wall and toss that colored ball. You're at DEF CON, damn it-wake up!"
p.STANZA.COORDINATE.body=STANZA.COORDINATE\n\nCompare the numbers on the sheet with the room: corners, distances, objects. You move slowly, measuring with your eyes before your hands. You place the sheet on the edge of the table and a small, unexpected gust of wind blows it to the back of the table. You run your fingers over it; it is narrow, full of dust, and you try to avoid the cables. Then you notice a detail: a screw is loose by a quarter turn. When you touch it, the metal is warmer than the rest. Someone has been there recently. Or is that spot close to something that works? Nah... the computer is quite far from there. Who was there just before you? Was the screw really hot, or was it just your imagination? You feel watched, confused.
p.STANZA.COORDINATE.c1=Unscrew it and see what's behind it.
p.STANZA.COORDINATE.c2=Leave everything as it is and go to the terminal
p.STANZA.DEFCON-BADGE.body=STANZA.DEFCON-BADGE\n\nYou crouch toward the "tick" as if you were tracking an electronic insect. The neon hums above you, and under the table the light turns slanted, sliced up by cables and the chair legs. The air down here smells like components: plastic, dust, a hint of warmed metal.\n\nThe sound is coming from something hanging there, but it's so dark you can't even see it.\n\nA black plate, about the size of a palm, cut into a shape that's neither square nor round: a wide diamond with sharp corners and a kind of geometric "eye" at the top, like a crosshair. Two metal mounts hold it on a bright green lanyard. The noise was coming from right there- it was lifting slightly and tapping a metal bit against the locker beside which it was hiding.\n\nThe surface isn't plastic: it's a black printed circuit board, with faint traces running like thin veins. Around the edge, a crown of small square windows: LEDs set in like teeth. Some off, others looking ready to burst into light the moment someone wakes them.\n\nIn the center, the strangest thing: a round display, black, glossy, recessed like the pupil of an animal. Under the glass you glimpse a screen ready to appear, but… it doesn't.\n\n"Is it dead?" you think.\n\nA little farther right there's a small lever, but you don't even have time to take it in before a sudden racket erupts from a door you'd never noticed before.\n\nPeople shouting. You don't recognize the voices, but they're making so much noise it feels like they must have been there all along-yet you'd swear you only heard them just now.
p.STANZA.DEFCON-BADGE.c1=Pull down the lever
p.STANZA.DEFCON-BADGE.c2=Hang up the badge and approach the door, trying to listen to what these people have to say.
p.STANZA.FOGLIO.RETRO.body=STANZA.FOGLIO.RETRO\n\nTurn the page. Against the light, faint numbers written in pencil appear. They look like coordinates, but they don't make sense: they are too specific to be random, too crooked to be printed.
p.STANZA.FOGLIO.RETRO.c1=Look for number matches in the room
p.STANZA.FOGLIO.body=The paper is heavier than it looks, and rough to the touch: thick paper, poorly printed, as if the printer had struggled before spitting it out. \n\nMove the sheet toward the light of the monitor: the smudged ink creates halos, and the halos seem almost-but only almost-intentional.\n\nI notice that it is torn, but no pieces seem to be missing, it is simply battered.\ A series of lines, divided as if it were a poem, fill it, but yes, it is a poem. "" I like to think (and the sooner the better!) of a cybernetic meadow where mammals and computers coexist in programming harmony like pure water touching the clear sky. n\nI like to think (right now, please!) of a cybernetic forest full of pine trees and electronics where deer stroll peacefully among computers of the past as if they were flowers with rotating petals.\ n\nI like to think (it must be!) of a cybernetic ecology where we are free from our labors and joined with nature, restored to our mammalian brothers and sisters, and all watched over by machines of loving grace.\n\n- Richard Brautigan 1967\n"""
p.STANZA.FOGLIO.c1=Look for hidden writing on the back of the sheet.
p.STANZA.FOGLIO.c2=Hold the sheet and return to the terminal
p.STANZA.TICCHETTIO.body=STANZA.TICCHETTIO\n\nYou stop. And suddenly the room becomes noisy. The noise was actually already there: you were covering it up with the keyboard. The neon light vibrates slightly. The monitor illuminates your hands. On the screen, the cursor flashes with irritating patience. Somewhere, an electronic device ticks at regular intervals. It's not a digital beep, it's more of a metallic, almost mechanical sound. An artificial heart, but in bad taste.
p.STANZA.TICCHETTIO.c1=Go back to the computer and try to find a valid command.
p.STANZA.TICCHETTIO.c2=Come closer to the metallic ticking
p.STANZA.VITE.body=The screw gives way with a satisfying little "click." Under the table is a thin box secured with cable ties. The plastic shows signs of age and fingerprints: it's dull where someone has opened and closed it too many times. Inside: a short cable and an adapter labeled "GOL." You turn it over in your fingers. The connector is "right" in a way you recognize without knowing why: like a lock that is already waiting for you.
p.STANZA.VITE.c1=Insert the cable
p.STANZA.VITE.c2=Leave everything as it is and go to the terminal
p.TERMINAL.0.body=TERMINAL.0\n\nYour fingers return to the keyboard with an ancient automatism: input → output, question → answer. However, the terminal does not do you the favor of giving you directions. No title, no banner, no "welcome." Just a blank line. Press Enter. Only one thing appears: It's too little to be an interface, too precise to be an error. It's not a normal prompt. It's a pointing finger. It's the system saying, "Show me how you think."
p.TERMINAL.0.c1=Continue typing, trying to guess a valid command.
p.TERMINAL.0.c2=Stop and observe the room before proceeding
p.TERMINAL.1.body=TERMINAL.1\n\nYour fingers move before you have a plan. The cursor hypnotizes you, and you... talk to it. 'Is anyone there?' Enter. For a second you feel ridiculous, then the screen responds with a curt, impersonal rejection. It doesn't insult you, it doesn't explain: it puts you in your place. n\n'unknown command: "Hello"'\n'(hint: type "help" to see what you can do)'\n\nThe cursor reappears immediately, more stubborn than before.\n\nYou didn't get an answer. You got a rule: in here, words only count if they're in the right vocabulary.
p.TERMINAL.1.c1=Type help and send
p.TERMINAL.1.c2=Try to exit the terminal to explore more on that computer.
p.TERMINAL.BASE.body=TERMINAL.BASE\n\nThe fingers return to the keyboard as if nothing had happened. Again that flashing character, and nothing more.\n\n">"
p.TERMINAL.BASE.c1=Type help
p.TERMINAL.BASE.c2=Type nautilus
p.TERMINAL.HELP.body=Type 'help'.\n\nPress enter and wait a few seconds for something to happen. No graphical menu, no help guide, just three words lined up. Available commands: - help - frogger - nautilus Three possibilities. No explanation. As if the only form of trust granted here were: "try it and see what happens."
p.TERMINAL.HELP.c1=Type frogger
p.TERMINAL.HELP.c2=Type nautilus
